<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hippocrates of Chios</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 470–c. 410 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hippocrates of Chios was a Greek mathematician who worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> in the fifth century BCE. He wrote the first known systematic Elements of geometry, a forerunner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euclid">Euclid</a>'s, and is famous for showing that certain figures bounded by circular arcs, the lunes, have areas equal to rectilinear figures. He also reduced the problem of doubling the cube to finding two mean proportionals.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hippocrates-of-chios.jpg" alt="Hippocrates of Chios" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Audriusa · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hipocrat_arcs.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hippocrates of Chios was born on the island of Chios, where he was originally a merchant. After misadventures, in which he was robbed by either <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> or fraudulent customs officials, he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, possibly for litigation, and there became a leading mathematician. On Chios he may have been a pupil of the mathematician and astronomer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oenopides">Oenopides of Chios</a>. His mathematics probably shows some Pythagorean influence too, perhaps through contacts between Chios and the neighbouring island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a>, a centre of Pythagorean thinking, and he has been described as a para-Pythagorean, a philosophical fellow traveller.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His major accomplishment was to write the first systematically organised geometry textbook, the Elements (Stoicheia), setting out basic theorems as building blocks of mathematical theory. It gave mathematicians a common framework to build on. Only one fragment survives, embedded in the work of Simplicius, in which the areas of some lunes, now called Hippocratic lunes, are calculated. This belonged to a programme to square the circle. Hippocrates failed in that, but he was the first to prove an equality of area between a curved shape and a polygonal one. Ferdinand von Lindemann showed in 1882 that the approach could not succeed.</p><p>In the century after him at least four other mathematicians wrote their own Elements, improving terminology and logical structure, and his work was the basis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euclids-elements">Euclid's Elements</a> of about 325 BCE. He is believed to have originated the use of letters for the points and figures of a proposition, as in triangle ABC, and the use of power for the square of a line. He found a way to approach the duplication of the cube, the problem of constructing a cube root, and invented the technique of reduction, which turns a specific problem into a more general one that is easier to solve.</p><p>In astronomy he tried to explain comets and the Milky Way. His ideas are not clearly handed down, but he probably thought both were optical illusions, caused by the refraction of sunlight by moisture exhaled by a putative planet near the Sun and by the stars. He also held that light rays originate in the eye rather than in the object seen.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hippocrates of Chios” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Later Chians included the tragedian Ion, the mathematician <b>Hippocrates of Chios</b> and the historian Theopompus.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chios">Chios · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Chios, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.4,26.02&amp;year=-431" aria-label="Chios on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5197 785 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Hippocrates of Chios"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5647 1055h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5647 1055h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.4,26.02&amp;year=-431">What was here in 431 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
